; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Reverse a Word
; CATEGORY: Recursion
; LEVEL:    Beginner
; TAGS:     recursion, words, letters, input
; SUMMARY:  Turns words back to front with a short recursive procedure and lets you type your own words to flip.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   To reverse "turtle, reverse "urtle and put the "t on the end.
;   That is the whole idea of BACKWARDS. The word gets one letter
;   shorter at each call until it is empty, and an empty word reversed
;   is still empty. The program shows some examples, then asks you for
;   words to reverse until you type quit.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   1. Press Run. Type a word in the command box and press Enter.
;      Type quit to finish.
;   2. To look inside, put a breakpoint on the line
;        output word backwards butfirst :w first :w
;      and press F5. In the Variables panel :w is the whole word. Press
;      F5 again and again: :w loses its first letter each time.
;   3. In the Call Stack, click each BACKWARDS from the newest to the
;      oldest. Each one is waiting to stick its own first letter on the
;      end of the answer it gets back.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * FIRST and BUTFIRST work on words as well as lists
;   * WORD glues letters together
;   * reading input with READWORD in a loop
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Write SHOUTY :w that puts a - between every letter: t-u-r-t-l-e.
;   * Reverse a whole sentence word by word, using a list instead.
;   * Type racecar. What is special about it? (See palindromes.lgo.)
; ============================================================

to backwards :w
  if emptyp :w [output "]
  output word backwards butfirst :w first :w
end

; Shows each step of the unfolding as a little equation, so a reader without
; the debugger can still see the pattern.
to explain :w
  ; a single letter is its own reverse, so the explanation ends there
  if emptyp butfirst :w [print (sentence "backwards :w "= :w) stop]
  print (sentence "backwards :w "= "backwards (butfirst :w) "+ first :w)
  explain butfirst :w
end

to ask.for.words
  print []
  print [Type a word to reverse, or quit to stop:]
  localmake "w readword
  if equalp :w "quit [print [Bye!] stop]
  ; an empty line gives an empty word: ask again
  ifelse emptyp :w [print [That was empty.]] [
    print (sentence :w "-> backwards :w)
  ]
  ask.for.words
end

foreach [turtle logo stressed drawer] [print (sentence ? "-> backwards ?)]
print []
print [How it works for "logo:]
explain "logo
ask.for.words
